1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,415
You change a database setting,

2
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:03,628
restart the server,

3
00:00:03,628 --> 00:00:04,969
and the data is gone.

4
00:00:04,969 --> 00:00:07,076
Or the setting never took effect.

5
00:00:07,076 --> 00:00:09,119
Both happen to teams every week.

6
00:00:09,669 --> 00:00:13,096
Today you configure a real post gress server the safe way,

7
00:00:13,096 --> 00:00:15,578
and you prove its data survives a restart.

8
00:00:16,128 --> 00:00:18,848
Three ideas: settings in a drop-in file,

9
00:00:18,848 --> 00:00:20,344
reload versus restart,

10
00:00:20,344 --> 00:00:22,656
and one data folder that persists.

11
00:00:23,206 --> 00:00:25,809
Configure it. Restart it. Prove it persists.

12
00:00:27,359 --> 00:00:30,431
Here is what to expect, from the first minute to the last.

13
00:00:30,981 --> 00:00:32,068
One: the words.

14
00:00:32,068 --> 00:00:32,938
Config file,

15
00:00:32,938 --> 00:00:33,518
drop-in,

16
00:00:33,518 --> 00:00:34,025
reload,

17
00:00:34,025 --> 00:00:34,605
restart,

18
00:00:34,605 --> 00:00:35,693
data directory,

19
00:00:35,693 --> 00:00:37,360
and persistent storage.

20
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:43,307
Two: a live demo on real post gress. We look at the defaults and where the data lives.

21
00:00:43,857 --> 00:00:45,683
Three: we write a drop-in file,

22
00:00:45,683 --> 00:00:46,096
reload,

23
00:00:46,096 --> 00:00:48,334
see which settings wait for a restart,

24
00:00:48,334 --> 00:00:49,041
and restart.

25
00:00:49,591 --> 00:00:51,588
Four: we prove the data survived.

26
00:00:51,588 --> 00:00:52,496
Then your turn,

27
00:00:52,496 --> 00:00:55,159
with a lab that judges you with five checks.

28
00:00:55,709 --> 00:00:58,354
This is lesson three of the databases domain.

29
00:00:58,904 --> 00:01:01,507
Configure it. Restart it. Prove it persists.

30
00:01:03,557 --> 00:01:06,352
Six ideas for configuring a database server.

31
00:01:06,902 --> 00:01:10,361
The main config file is post gress Q L dot conf.

32
00:01:10,361 --> 00:01:11,442
Do not edit it.

33
00:01:11,442 --> 00:01:14,756
Add your own settings in a small drop-in file,

34
00:01:14,756 --> 00:01:16,558
in the conf dot D folder.

35
00:01:16,558 --> 00:01:18,071
It is easy to review,

36
00:01:18,071 --> 00:01:19,296
and easy to undo.

37
00:01:19,846 --> 00:01:21,692
Every setting has a context.

38
00:01:21,692 --> 00:01:23,340
Some apply with a reload.

39
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:23,669
Some,

40
00:01:23,669 --> 00:01:26,306
like max connections and shared buffers,

41
00:01:26,306 --> 00:01:27,624
need a full restart.

42
00:01:27,624 --> 00:01:30,129
The P G settings view tells you which.

43
00:01:30,679 --> 00:01:34,235
A reload re-reads the files and keeps every connection.

44
00:01:34,235 --> 00:01:35,980
A restart stops the server,

45
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:37,273
and starts it again.

46
00:01:37,273 --> 00:01:39,212
Use a reload whenever you can.

47
00:01:39,762 --> 00:01:41,028
The data directory,

48
00:01:41,028 --> 00:01:42,094
called P G data,

49
00:01:42,094 --> 00:01:43,293
holds every table,

50
00:01:43,293 --> 00:01:43,960
every row,

51
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:45,559
and the write ahead log.

52
00:01:45,559 --> 00:01:47,890
It is the only thing you must keep.

53
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:53,420
Persistent storage means that folder lives on a disk that outlives the server.

54
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:54,378
In a container,

55
00:01:54,378 --> 00:01:55,336
it is a volume.

56
00:01:55,336 --> 00:01:57,251
A restart must never touch it.

57
00:01:57,801 --> 00:02:00,226
The data folder belongs to one user,

58
00:02:00,226 --> 00:02:02,315
and is closed to everyone else.

59
00:02:02,315 --> 00:02:06,761
Persistence is not a backup: a deleted disk still means lost data.

60
00:02:08,311 --> 00:02:13,367
Go to the lab folder.

61
00:02:14,917 --> 00:02:16,816
Look around. Type: L S.

62
00:02:17,366 --> 00:02:18,280
A data folder,

63
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:19,129
a server log,

64
00:02:19,129 --> 00:02:20,174
and a task file.

65
00:02:20,174 --> 00:02:22,329
The post gress server is running,

66
00:02:22,329 --> 00:02:23,766
from that data folder.

67
00:02:24,316 --> 00:02:32,550
Where is the data?

68
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:33,774
Slash opt,

69
00:02:33,774 --> 00:02:35,594
slash lab one twenty-eight,

70
00:02:35,594 --> 00:02:36,336
slash data.

71
00:02:36,336 --> 00:02:38,897
This is the persistent data directory.

72
00:02:38,897 --> 00:02:42,402
Everything the database knows is in this one folder.

73
00:02:42,952 --> 00:02:47,005
What is inside?

74
00:02:47,555 --> 00:02:49,189
Base holds the databases.

75
00:02:49,189 --> 00:02:51,934
There are folders for the write ahead log,

76
00:02:51,934 --> 00:02:53,307
and the config files.

77
00:02:53,307 --> 00:02:56,771
And a folder I made for drop-in settings: conf dot D.

78
00:02:57,321 --> 00:03:15,369
Read the three settings.

79
00:03:15,919 --> 00:03:18,029
Max connections is one hundred.

80
00:03:18,029 --> 00:03:21,229
Look at the context column: it says postmaster,

81
00:03:21,229 --> 00:03:23,203
meaning this needs a restart.

82
00:03:23,203 --> 00:03:25,246
Shared buffers also needs one.

83
00:03:25,246 --> 00:03:27,560
Log min duration statement is off,

84
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:28,241
minus one,

85
00:03:28,241 --> 00:03:31,236
and a superuser can change it with a reload.

86
00:03:31,786 --> 00:03:40,661
Write the answer.

87
00:03:41,211 --> 00:03:47,014
Create the drop-in file.

88
00:03:47,564 --> 00:03:48,383
Three lines.

89
00:03:48,383 --> 00:03:49,476
Max connections,

90
00:03:49,476 --> 00:03:49,886
fifty.

91
00:03:49,886 --> 00:03:50,910
Shared buffers,

92
00:03:50,910 --> 00:03:52,344
sixty-four megabytes.

93
00:03:52,344 --> 00:03:54,461
And log min duration statement,

94
00:03:54,461 --> 00:03:55,690
two hundred fifty.

95
00:03:55,690 --> 00:03:59,173
That logs every query slower than a quarter second.

96
00:03:59,173 --> 00:04:00,129
Save the file.

97
00:04:04,479 --> 00:04:09,386
Read it back. Type: cat, data, slash, conf dot D, slash, app dot conf.

98
00:04:09,936 --> 00:04:21,370
Reload the server.

99
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:24,288
True. No restart, no dropped connection.

100
00:04:24,838 --> 00:04:41,094
What applied?

101
00:04:41,644 --> 00:04:45,110
Log min duration statement is now two hundred fifty.

102
00:04:45,110 --> 00:04:49,442
But max connections and shared buffers still show the old values,

103
00:04:49,442 --> 00:04:51,309
and pending restart is true.

104
00:04:51,309 --> 00:04:52,908
They wait for a restart.

105
00:04:53,458 --> 00:05:08,050
Add a row first.

106
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:10,968
Insert, zero, one. One row added.

107
00:05:11,518 --> 00:05:20,926
Restart the server.

108
00:05:21,476 --> 00:05:23,450
Waiting for server to shut down,

109
00:05:23,450 --> 00:05:24,622
and server started.

110
00:05:24,622 --> 00:05:26,966
The process stopped and started again.

111
00:05:26,966 --> 00:05:28,755
Everything in memory is gone.

112
00:05:28,755 --> 00:05:29,988
What about the data?

113
00:05:30,538 --> 00:05:47,136
Did settings apply?

114
00:05:47,686 --> 00:05:49,363
Max connections is fifty.

115
00:05:49,363 --> 00:05:54,528
Shared buffers is eight thousand one hundred ninety-two eight-kilobyte pages,

116
00:05:54,528 --> 00:05:56,541
which is sixty-four megabytes.

117
00:05:56,541 --> 00:05:58,218
Pending restart is false.

118
00:05:58,218 --> 00:05:59,291
All three apply.

119
00:05:59,841 --> 00:06:06,518
Is the data there?

120
00:06:07,068 --> 00:06:07,705
Both rows.

121
00:06:07,705 --> 00:06:08,660
The first note,

122
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:09,487
and survives.

123
00:06:09,487 --> 00:06:11,652
The data lives in the data folder,

124
00:06:11,652 --> 00:06:13,752
and the restart did not touch it.

125
00:06:13,752 --> 00:06:15,026
That is persistence.

126
00:06:15,576 --> 00:06:28,504
Prove the restart happened.

127
00:06:29,054 --> 00:06:31,528
A start time after the note was saved.

128
00:06:31,528 --> 00:06:32,701
The server is new.

129
00:06:32,701 --> 00:06:33,743
The data is old.

130
00:06:33,743 --> 00:06:35,240
Exactly what we wanted.

131
00:06:35,790 --> 00:06:38,158
Run the judge.

132
00:06:38,708 --> 00:06:44,191
Five checks. Counted, restarted, reloaded, drop-in, and persisted. Five out of five.

133
00:06:44,741 --> 00:06:46,704
Clean up.

134
00:06:47,254 --> 00:06:49,920
The server is stopped and everything is removed.

135
00:06:50,470 --> 00:06:58,982
Is it gone?

136
00:06:59,532 --> 00:07:02,050
No response. Nothing is left behind.

137
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:07,205
Your task. Configure the server, and prove its data persists.

138
00:07:07,755 --> 00:07:10,155
One: read the default max connections,

139
00:07:10,155 --> 00:07:12,365
and write it into answers dot text,

140
00:07:12,365 --> 00:07:14,070
before you change anything.

141
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:16,537
Two: create the drop-in file,

142
00:07:16,537 --> 00:07:18,323
with max connections fifty,

143
00:07:18,323 --> 00:07:20,703
shared buffers sixty-four megabytes,

144
00:07:20,703 --> 00:07:23,943
and log min duration statement two hundred fifty.

145
00:07:24,493 --> 00:07:29,549
Three: reload, and see which setting applied, and which waits. Add a note called survives.

146
00:07:30,099 --> 00:07:31,637
Four: restart the server.

147
00:07:31,637 --> 00:07:33,175
Five: check the settings,

148
00:07:33,175 --> 00:07:35,205
check both notes are still there,

149
00:07:35,205 --> 00:07:36,128
then lab check,

150
00:07:36,128 --> 00:07:36,989
then lab down.

151
00:07:39,039 --> 00:07:42,815
Here is where to find everything. Pause on this screen, and write it down.

152
00:07:43,365 --> 00:07:45,013
The lab lives on GitHub,

153
00:07:45,013 --> 00:07:46,180
at it dash waves,

154
00:07:46,180 --> 00:07:47,760
slash dev ops dash A I,

155
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:49,270
in the folder academy,

156
00:07:49,270 --> 00:07:49,957
slash lab.

157
00:07:50,507 --> 00:07:56,054
Two files: lab one twenty-eight dot S H, and shortcuts one twenty-eight dot S H.

158
00:07:56,604 --> 00:07:57,491
Install it,

159
00:07:57,491 --> 00:08:00,314
on any Linux machine: sudo install,

160
00:08:00,314 --> 00:08:02,169
dash m seven five five,

161
00:08:02,169 --> 00:08:04,509
lab one twenty-eight dot S H,

162
00:08:04,509 --> 00:08:05,718
into slash usr,

163
00:08:05,718 --> 00:08:06,686
slash local,

164
00:08:06,686 --> 00:08:07,654
slash S bin.

165
00:08:08,204 --> 00:08:13,275
Load the short names with: source shortcuts one twenty-eight dot S H.

166
00:08:13,275 --> 00:08:14,671
You get three: lab,

167
00:08:14,671 --> 00:08:14,965
P G,

168
00:08:14,965 --> 00:08:16,140
and P G control.

169
00:08:16,690 --> 00:08:18,307
You need post gress Q L,

170
00:08:18,307 --> 00:08:19,519
server and client,

171
00:08:19,519 --> 00:08:20,193
installed,

172
00:08:20,193 --> 00:08:22,618
and port fifty-four eighty-one free.

173
00:08:22,618 --> 00:08:25,313
The lab runs its own private post gress,

174
00:08:25,313 --> 00:08:26,525
in its own folder.

175
00:08:27,075 --> 00:08:28,782
Lab up prepares everything.

176
00:08:28,782 --> 00:08:30,489
Lab check judges your work.

177
00:08:30,489 --> 00:08:32,006
Lab down removes it all.

178
00:08:32,006 --> 00:08:34,030
Only run it on your own machine.

179
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:38,149
Pass or fail. You need all five.

180
00:08:38,699 --> 00:08:41,473
One: you read the default max connections first.

181
00:08:42,023 --> 00:08:46,746
Two: max connections is fifty and shared buffers is sixty-four megabytes,

182
00:08:46,746 --> 00:08:47,911
after the restart.

183
00:08:48,461 --> 00:08:53,111
Three: log min duration statement is two hundred fifty, after the reload.

184
00:08:53,661 --> 00:08:58,291
Four: the settings are in the drop-in file, and the main config file is untouched.

185
00:08:58,841 --> 00:09:02,062
Five: the note you saved before the restart is still there.

186
00:09:02,612 --> 00:09:03,913
To pass, all five.

187
00:09:05,963 --> 00:09:07,734
Your turn, on your own machine.

188
00:09:08,284 --> 00:09:10,993
One: lab up, read the defaults, and write the answer.

189
00:09:11,543 --> 00:09:14,316
Two: create the drop-in file with the three settings.

190
00:09:14,866 --> 00:09:18,258
Three: reload, look at pending restart, and insert the note.

191
00:09:18,808 --> 00:09:21,987
Four: restart, and check the settings and both notes.

192
00:09:22,537 --> 00:09:26,356
Five: lab check until you see five out of five. Then lab down.

193
00:09:26,906 --> 00:09:28,740
Pause the video, and begin now.

194
00:09:44,290 --> 00:09:45,165
Quick check.

195
00:09:45,715 --> 00:09:49,149
Why use a drop-in file instead of editing the main config?

196
00:09:49,699 --> 00:09:52,366
What is the difference between a reload and a restart?

197
00:09:52,916 --> 00:09:55,135
Which two settings needed a restart today?

198
00:09:55,685 --> 00:09:59,055
What makes storage persistent, and what does it not protect you from?

199
00:09:59,605 --> 00:10:02,056
Objectives covered: M C fourteen,

200
00:10:02,056 --> 00:10:03,170
objective four.

201
00:10:03,170 --> 00:10:07,328
Database configuration and persistent storage for a lab.

202
00:10:07,878 --> 00:10:09,947
